==============================================================================
PART A/B -- THE SCORE-PARITY LAW AND THE CANONICAL REPRESENTATIVE
==============================================================================

 n=3  (n mod 4 = 3)   C(n,2) = 3  -> #odd scores is ODD
   parity law holds on all 2^n switchings : True
   distinct parity vectors per switching class : [4]   (2^(n-1) = 4 predicted for n odd)
   #reps with ALL SCORES EVEN  : [0]     #reps with exactly ONE odd score : [3]

 n=4  (n mod 4 = 0)   C(n,2) = 6  -> #odd scores is EVEN
   parity law holds on all 2^n switchings : True
   distinct parity vectors per switching class : [2]   (2^(n-1) = 8 predicted for n odd)
   #reps with ALL SCORES EVEN  : [0, 4]     #reps with exactly ONE odd score : [0]

 n=5  (n mod 4 = 1)   C(n,2) = 10  -> #odd scores is EVEN
   parity law holds on all 2^n switchings : True
   distinct parity vectors per switching class : [16]   (2^(n-1) = 16 predicted for n odd)
   #reps with ALL SCORES EVEN  : [1]     #reps with exactly ONE odd score : [0]

 n=6  (n mod 4 = 2)   C(n,2) = 15  -> #odd scores is ODD
   parity law holds on all 2^n switchings : True
   distinct parity vectors per switching class : [2]   (2^(n-1) = 32 predicted for n odd)
   #reps with ALL SCORES EVEN  : [0]     #reps with exactly ONE odd score : [0, 16]

 n=7  (n mod 4 = 3)   C(n,2) = 21  -> #odd scores is ODD
   parity law holds on all 2^n switchings : True
   distinct parity vectors per switching class : [64]   (2^(n-1) = 64 predicted for n odd)
   #reps with ALL SCORES EVEN  : [0]     #reps with exactly ONE odd score : [7]

==============================================================================
PART C -- THE COUNT: switching classes up to iso  vs  EVEN TOURNAMENTS up to iso
==============================================================================
  n  n%4  iso classes  switch cls /iso  even tourns /iso   A049313   match?
  3    3            2                1                 0         1      n/a
  4    0            4                2                 1         2      n/a
  5    1           12                2                 2         2     True
  6    2           56                6                 0         6      n/a
  7    3          456               12                 0        12      n/a

 n=7 (= 3 mod 4): every switching class has exactly 7 single-odd-score reps.
 Counting switching classes up to iso directly is 2^21 x 5040 and is not run here.
 What IS run: the representative counts above already confirm 'exactly 7' at n=7.

==============================================================================
PART D -- THE ODD-VALUED SIDE: where the project's parity actually lives
==============================================================================
  n         det S(T) over 200 random T
  3   n ODD   distinct dets: [0]   all zero
  4   n EVEN  distinct dets: [Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(9, 1)]   all perfect squares (= Pf^2): True
  5   n ODD   distinct dets: [0]   all zero
  6   n EVEN  distinct dets: [Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(9, 1), Fraction(25, 1), Fraction(49, 1), Fraction(81, 1)]   all perfect squares (= Pf^2): True
  7   n ODD   distinct dets: [0]   all zero
  8   n EVEN  distinct dets: [Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(9, 1), Fraction(25, 1), Fraction(49, 1), Fraction(81, 1), Fraction(121, 1)]...   all perfect squares (= Pf^2): True

 READING: skew-symmetric of ODD order is singular, so det S(T) = 0 for every
 tournament on an odd number of vertices; for EVEN order det S = Pf(S)^2 is a square.
 That is the same odd/even split that makes the SCORE-PARITY law work only at odd n,
 and it is the matrix-level shadow of Redei's theorem that hp(T) is always ODD.

DONE.

